  JSON-to-source step imported the ICurry of a packaged module without its
  package.  The step imports the package first now.  Measured, CPU time in
  new processes after a fresh stage (make unstage, make stage): the stage
  took 43.8 s user and 5.1 s system, most of it the Prelude compile of the
  C++ backend; the first Prelude import 0.032 and 0.027 s on the Python
  backend (0.226 s before, with the source compile) and 0.038 and 0.039 s
  on the C++ backend; zero compiler commands and zero source compiles on
  both (hooks on _system.pexec and SourceFileLoader.source_to_code); import
  curry costs 0.05 to 0.065 s on top.  Reading the Prelude cache costs 3 ms
  against 196 ms for the source (prototype on a copy of Prelude.py).  A
  stage that changes nothing keeps every product (mtimes unchanged).
  Tests: unit_prebuild.py (the staged products of both backends, a first
  import that compiles nothing in a child process, the cache written under
  -B, the loader reading it and compiling again after a change,
  ensure_bytecode, an unwritable cache, --tidy, a module in a package),
  unit_make.py (--so), unit_cxx_toolchain.py (TestMissingCompiler).  Left:
  a generated .py carries no format stamp, so a change to the Python
  emitter does not invalidate a cached Prelude.py (the C++ side has
  FORMAT_VERSION); the prebuild keeps a current .py.  The flavor of the
  precompiled header built at stage time follows the flags of the stage; a
  run with CXXFLAGS set builds its own member at its first compile.  CI
  pays the Prelude compile in the make stage of every job now (about 30 s),
  which the first test import paid before; the py job pays it too.
- Content-keyed ICurry cache (issue #12).  The Curry-to-ICurry cache was off
  by default and could not hit for a dynamic module: its table was named
  after CURRYPATH, which starts with a fresh temporary directory for every
  curry.compile, and its key held the module name, which curry.compile
  numbers per process.  So every unit test paid the front end (about 5 s per
  module; unit_compile.py: 21 calls, 128 s on the Python backend).  Done:
  the key (cache.icurry_cache_key) is a SHA-256 of the module source, the
  sources of the modules it imports (transitively, found as .curry files in
  the Curry path and beside the file; the Prelude is implicit; a module that
  is nowhere, such as a library of PAKCS, adds nothing), the front-end
  options (none today), the icurry tool itself, and the intermediate
  subdirectory, which names the library version.  The file name is not part
  of the key.  The module name is part of it for a named module, because the
  front end writes it into the ICurry; an anonymous module
  (sprite__interactive_N, sprite__expression_N; config.is_anonymous_modname)
  leaves it out, and the cached text is renamed on a hit
  (cache.rename_module: the name follows a double quote or a hash sign and
  precedes a double quote or a dot; verified against the front end's own
  output for another name on a module with instances, a superclass, a
  default method, lambdas, local functions, and an external: 133
  occurrences, equal byte for byte).  A program error of the front end (an
  error at a source position) is cached too and replayed as the same
  CompileError with the new name; a failure of the environment is not
  cached.  SPRITE_CACHE_FILE now names the file and turns the cache on; the
  empty string turns it off; unset, Make.config decides as before.  The test
  drivers (tests/run_tests and the CI script) set tests/.cache/icurry.db,
  and the workflow restores and saves tests/.cache with actions/cache
  (restore keys: the same job, then any job; saved after the tests even when
  one failed).  SPRITE_CACHE_UPDATE matches the Curry source file name.
  The rows live in table curry2icurry_2 (key, name, text, error, created);
  a database error is a warning and the front end runs.  The parsed-JSON
  cache is unchanged.  Found on the way: _system.pexec now attaches
  command, returncode, and stderr to its CompileError.  Measured on the
  Python backend, CPU time of the test process and its children:
  unit_compile.py cold with the cache: 11 tests, 21 front end calls,
  143.7 s user and 42.6 s system (the machine was shared; the same file
  without the cache took 94.6 s user and 33.4 s system earlier in the day,
  21 calls as well); the second run: 0 front end calls, 21 hits (one of
  them the replayed program error), 0.67 s user and 0.09 s system, 0.6 s of
  wall time.  A hit costs about 0.01 s of CPU in curry.compile; the key
  alone costs 2.3 ms (it reads and digests the source and its import
  closure every time: four library modules, 87 KB, for an expression that
  imports Data.List and Control.SetFunctions), and the digest of the icurry
  tool 2 ms once per process.  Tests: unit_cache.py (the rename, the key
  and the import closure, the slot without the front end, the error replay,
  curry.compile hits in the process and in a second process, a named module
  through the toolchain).  Left: the import scan
  is a regular expression over import declarations, lenient on purpose; a
  module present only as .icy or .json.z adds nothing to the key; entries
  never expire (delete the file); the PAKCS version is covered by the
  subdirectory, not by a digest of PAKCS; the old tables of an existing
  ~/.sprite/cache.db stay in the file.
- Bytecode cache for generated Python modules (issue #13).  The prebuild
  entry above covers the Prelude and the system modules: the toolchain
  writes the cache when it writes a .py file, and the loader reads it
  through importlib.  Left open there: a generated file without a cache,
  such as a test module from the overlay archive or from an installation
  made before the cache was written, was compiled from source in every
  process, because the test drivers run Python with -B and importlib writes
  no cache then (the 1,151 generated test modules under tests/data had no
  __pycache__ at all).  Done: the loader writes a missing or stale cache
  (ensure_bytecode) before it loads the file, -B or not; the cache is a
  product of the toolchain, not of an import.  So a generated module is
  compiled from source at most once per change, in any process.  A cache
  that cannot be written gives one warning and the file loads from source.
  Measured on the Python backend, new processes, CPU time: the Prelude
  import costs 0.026 to 0.035 s with zero source compiles (import curry
  0.064 to 0.076 s before it).  unit_py_evaluation.py with a warm ICurry
  cache, CPU of the test process after the imports, whole process in
  parentheses (user plus system): 1.23 s (1.34 to 1.35 s) with the loader of
  the committed tree, which ran the file through runpy and compiled the
  Prelude six times (once per hard reset) and each test module once; 0.26
  to 0.27 s (0.37 s) with the cache of the Prelude alone, the five test
  modules still compiled in every process; 0.32 s (0.43 s) in the run that
  wrote their caches and 0.28 to 0.29 s (0.41 to 0.42 s) after it, zero
  source compiles of cached files.  The five modules are small (1 to 5 KB),
  so their compile cost is in the noise of that file; the gain of the loader
  change is that the larger test modules and the 1,151 modules of the
  functional suites are compiled once instead of once per process.  Tests:
  unit_prebuild.py (the loader writes a stale or missing cache under -B, a
  file without a cache is compiled by the first of two child processes only,
  an unwritable cache gives one warning through the loader).  Left: a
  generated .py still carries no format stamp (see the prebuild entry); a
  read-only tree whose caches are missing warns at every load.
- Run statistics (issue #8).  sprite-exec -t printed the wall time of the
  evaluation at 1 ms resolution and nothing else, and the counters of the
  runtimes (steps_total and gc_collections of the C++ runtime, the step
  counter and the telemetry object of the Python backend) reached no tool.
  Done: sprite-exec --stats prints one line of key=value pairs on stderr
  when the program ends, after its output and after an error message:
  wall (seconds since the process started, read from /proc on Linux, since
  the import of the package elsewhere), cpu (user plus system seconds of
  the process, children excluded), steps (rewrite steps), forks (forks of
  a choice-rooted configuration), collections (the node collector of the
  C++ backend; 0 on the Python backend), peak_rss (bytes, from getrusage),
  and compile (seconds spent in the steps of the toolchain: front end,
  JSON, code generator, C++ compiler; 0 when every file was current).  The
  same numbers come from curry.stats() (Interpreter.stats, module
  interpreter/stats.py): a dict with the seven keys in that order whose
  str is the line.  Steps and forks are per interpreter: the runtime state
  of an evaluation counts its own (steps_total and forks_total on both
  backends; forks_total is new in the C++ RuntimeState, counted in fork),
  and the generic evaluator sums them in EvaluationTotals: a running
  evaluation is held by a weak reference and counted as it goes; an
  evaluation whose generator ends or is dropped is added once.  No hot
  path changed on either backend.  The compile clock sits around the step
  loop of makecurry (outermost call only), so an import that finds every
  file current adds nothing.  The other fields are per process.  Measured
  on mynot (committed caches, no front end): the Python backend reports
  steps=3 forks=1 and the C++ backend steps=4 forks=1 (the backends count
  differently; compare steps within one backend); the first C++ run after
  the header change compiled mynot.so again and reported compile=0.62 s,
  the next run compile=0.000000; cpu about 0.12 s on both backends,
  peak_rss 35 to 37 MB.  Tests: unit_api.py (TestStats: the fields and
  the line, the counts of an evaluation and of a running one, the compile
  field on a hand-written module and on a current one, sprite-exec --stats
  after a run and after an error, no line without the option),
  unit_py_interp.py (testStats: totals per interpreter, kept across a soft
  reset), unit_cxx_gc.py (the collections field follows gc_collections).
  Left: a direct step through evaluator.single_step is added as well, but
  not tested; the harness (M1) is the consumer of the line.
- Benchmark harness v2 (issue #7).  The driver tests/lib/benchmark.py printed
  the best of N wall times of sprite-exec -t to the terminal and stored
  nothing; no suite measured compile time, start-up, or memory.  Done: the
  package tests/lib/benchmarks (benchmark.py forwards to it; ./run_benchmarks
  runs it) with four suites selected by name: throughput (the 30
  dissertation programs; sprite-exec -t --stats, or PAKCS with :set +time),
  compile (each program compiled from source in an empty directory with the
  ICurry cache cold and warm, and the expression 1+2 through curry.compile in
  a child Python), import (python -c pass, import curry, the Prelude import,
  Hello end to end), and memory (the collector on and, on the C++ backend,
  off).  Every measurement runs in a child under timeout and prlimit --as;
  N measured repetitions (5, 3, 5, 1 by suite) follow warm-up runs that are
  not recorded.  One JSON Lines record per item holds the medians of wall and
  CPU seconds (user plus system of the child and its descendants, from
  wait4), peak RSS, the evaluation time alone, the compile seconds, the exact
  counters steps, forks, and collections of --stats (equal in every
  repetition, else null with a warning), the samples, the commit with
  -dirty, the date, a label, and the machine facts.  The compare command
  joins two files on suite, program, backend, and variant, prints the ratio
  of one metric with a noise threshold (same, faster, slower) and the
  counters that changed, and fails under --strict.  Found on the way: Linux
  starts the memory high-water mark of a new process at the resident set of
  its parent, so wait4 on a child of the harness never reports a peak below
  the harness itself (34 MB inside the test process, where python -c pass
  takes 9 MB); the harness runs GNU time inside the wrapper chain for the
  peak, and falls back to wait4 with a note in the record when GNU time is
  missing.  Measured on a shared machine (one repetition, smoke test only;
  the baseline waits for a quiet machine): Hello costs 0.14 to 0.17 s of CPU
  per process on either backend and 1.7 s under PAKCS; repeated records
  agree exactly on steps (1) and forks (0); the warm module compile of Hello
  costs 0.035 s of toolchain time on the Python backend against 5.3 s cold
  (the front end).  Tests: unit_benchmarks.py (the runner with the cap, the
  timeout, a grandchild, and a fat parent; the parsers; the records; the
  suites and their commands; the options; compare on hand-made records; the
  run command end to end on Hello and on the expression).  Docs: tests/README
  section 8 and the README of the benchmark directory.  Left: the nightly
  job with history (M4), the baseline run (M5), a PAKCS column outside the
  throughput suite, in-process repetition for programs under 100 ms (the
  toofast directory stays out of the set), and node counts, which --stats
  does not report.
- Review fixes for the latency items.  ICurry cache: the import scan took
  only names with an uppercase first letter, so the import that curry.compile
  prepends for a dynamic module (import sprite__interactive_N) was not in the
  closure, and the key of an expression left out the source of that module.
  A second process, whose counter gives the same name to another module,
  replayed the expression compiled against the first (probed: show (f 3)
  raised a TypeError after f changed from Int -> Int to Int -> [Int]).  The
  scan accepts any identifier now, and the closure finds the dynamic module
  in the temporary directory that curry.compile puts first in the path.  The
  front end reports a span (line:col-line:col) for a name of more than one
  character; the error matcher took a point only, so most program errors
  were never cached.  It takes the span now.  A missing import ("Interface
  for module M not found") is not cached on purpose: the key sees the Curry
  path only through source files.  Prebuild: sprite-invoke keeps an exported
  SPRITE_HOME, so a make stage run with the variable set compiled the
  library of that installation.  The recipe sets SPRITE_HOME to PREFIX,
  unsets SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS, SPRITE_CXX_PCH_ROOT, and
  SPRITE_FORCE_RECOMPILE_CXX as well, and fails when Prelude.py (and, with a
  compiler, Prelude.so and its stamp) is missing under PREFIX afterwards.
  C++ toolchain: the format check of a .cpp file belongs to Json2Cpp, the
  step that writes it, so the plan of sprite-make --cxx refuses a stale file
  too (Cpp2So asks the same question; the plan asks a step about its input
  as well, so Json2Cpp answers for .cpp files only).  Cpp2So removes the old
  ABI stamp before g++ runs, so an interrupted compile leaves an object
  without a stamp, not one with the stamp of other headers.  runtime_digest
  raises a PrerequisiteError that names the installation when a header
  cannot be read (a staged link into a source tree that is gone) and gives
  None for a tree without headers; the objects are trusted then, because
  such an installation cannot compile.  Statistics: a step through
  evaluator.single_step (the first step of every compiled expression) was
  never counted on either backend, because the step functions count nothing
  themselves.  Both runtime states have single_step now: the Python one
  passes the root as a variable and counts as S does, the C++ binding counts
  a completed step as procS does.  A compiled expression costs one step in
  --stats on both backends.  CI: the ICurry cache is saved under a key that
  holds the run id, so the nightly run, which alone compiles the functional
  suites, saves as well; the save step is skipped when tests/.cache does not
  exist.  Docs: the wall field of --stats, the configure --cache icurry
  option, the missing-module rule.  Tests: unit_cache.py (the dynamic import
  in the key and across three processes, the span and the missing-module
  errors, lowercase names in the scan), unit_cxx_toolchain.py (the
  source-only plan, the interrupted compile, the header tree),
  unit_prebuild.py (the recipe environment through make print-PREBUILD_ENV,
  sprite-make --py on a current file), unit_api.py (the single step counts
  on both backends).  The long lines of the new tests and of the harness
  were wrapped.  Left: the three-process cache test costs four front-end
  calls (about 20 s); entries of the old point-only error form stay valid.
- Toolchain stream integrated (stream C onto streams A and B).  The
  vendored library under curry/lib, the FlatCurry-to-ICurry port, and the
  front-end route (the default; icurry is the configured fallback) now
  carry the Phase 0 work.  The content-keyed ICurry cache wraps both routes,
  and its key names the route and carries a digest of its program: the
  icurry tool, or the Curry front end with its flags and the sources of the
  port (cache.frontend_digest, icurry_cache_key, Slot(tool=)).  So an entry
  written by one route is never served to the other, and a change to the
  port misses the cache as a new front end does.  The prebuild of make
  stage runs on the vendored layout: sprite-make --json over all 22 modules
  (no run of Sprite writes into the installation for a library module),
  then --py and --so over the modules Sprite can compile; make uninstall
  removes every product, the per-package FlatCurry of the front end
  included; an install of curry/ alone (no sprite-make under PREFIX) skips
  the step with a notice.  Nine modules are left out
  (CURRYLIB_UNSUPPORTED_MODULES in Make.include, exported as
  sysconfig/currylib_unsupported_modules; config.unsupported_syslibs and
  supported_syslibs): seven have externals that no backend implements
  (Handle, IORef, getArgs, getCPUTime, the constants of
  Curry.Compiler.Distribution) or import such a module (Debug.Trace), and
  Test.Prop with Test.Prop.Types, which the importer cannot build: Test.Prop
  is a module and a package and imports its own submodule; the import by
  name walks the prefix Test.Prop and builds it again while its provisional
  module is loaded (the assertion in Json2TargetSource fires), and
  _provisionalModule binds a submodule into an IPackage mapping, which an
  IModule parent is not.  Both backends reject the same nine.  jq is gone
  from configure, the conda recipe, and the docs; the CI matrix keeps the
  icurry key (the py job without icurry, the cxx jobs with it) with the
  cache steps of Phase 0.  Found on the way: the flavor of the precompiled
  header is a digest of the compiler flags, and the include flag followed
  the spelling of SPRITE_HOME; make stage names the installation through
  the link install/ and run_tests through its real path, so the member
  built at stage time was never the one the tests used, and a staged tree
  held two O3 members.  The flag is spelled by its real path now.  The
  committed Control/SetFunctions.json.z under curry/lib predated the import
  order fix of the encoder and was regenerated from its .icy; the other
  seven pinned caches matched.  Measured: a make stage that changes nothing
  keeps all 132 products of the library and the header (mtimes unchanged)
  and runs the front end zero times.  Tests: unit_cache.py (the route in
  the key, the digests of both routes, the calls of either front end),
  unit_prebuild.py (the supported set; the ICurry and JSON of every module),
  unit_currylib.py (the source-only test puts the products and the bytecode
  cache back, in toolchain order, because the staleness rule compares
  ctimes, which a copy cannot preserve), unit_conda.py (no jq),
  unit_cxx_toolchain.py (the real-path include flag).
  Left: the nine unsupported modules; the old overlay directories
  curry/pakcs-3.2.0, curry/pakcs-3.3.0, and curry/pakcs-3.4.1 and the old
  source overlays curry/Control and curry/Data, which nothing references
  now and which can go with git rm.
